---

## 4. Retention Triggers

Data retention periods are measured from the following trigger events:

- **Customer data**: From the date of account closure or last transaction.
- **Employee data**: From the date of employment termination.
- **Correspondence**: From the date of last communication.
- **Consent records**: From the date consent was withdrawn or the relationship ended.

---

## 5. Secure Disposal Procedures

Upon expiry of the retention period, personal data shall be disposed of using the following methods:

| Medium | Disposal Method |
|--------|----------------|
| Digital files (disk) | Cryptographic erasure or NIST 800-88 compliant wiping |
| Database records | Secure DELETE with log purge; or pseudonymisation |
| Physical documents | Cross-cut shredding (DIN 66399 Level P-4 or higher) |
| Backup media | Degaussing or physical destruction |
| Cloud storage | Provider-certified deletion with confirmation |

Disposal is recorded in the **Data Disposal Register** maintained by {{dataOwner}}.

---

## 6. Exceptions and Legal Hold

Data subject to a **legal hold** (litigation, regulatory inquiry, audit) shall be excluded from routine disposal until the hold is lifted. Legal holds are issued by {{legalContact}} and documented in the Legal Hold Register.

---

## 7. Third-Party Processors

All data processors engaged by {{organizationName}} are contractually required to maintain equivalent retention periods and disposal standards. Processor compliance is reviewed {{processorReviewFrequency}}.

---

## 8. Policy Review

This policy is reviewed **annually** or upon significant changes to:
- Applicable law or regulation (including DPDP Act subordinate rules)
- Business processes involving personal data
- Data systems and storage infrastructure

**Next scheduled review:** {{nextReviewDate}}

